messages:
maria: quick q — I'm seeing schema/coercion errors when using the JS SDK func-call API with streaming output. Also hit a 401 mid-stream when our short-lived token refreshed. retries didn't seem to re-auth, stream just failed. anyone run into this?

ken: can you paste the minimal repro + error text? streaming + func-call is a tricky combo.

maria: here's the gist — calling `client.funcall` with streaming true. SDK sends chunks like `event: output_chunk
data: {"items": [...` and then at the end we try to coerce to our zod schema and blow up with `Unexpected token` or `ZodError`. also we saw a `401` from the server ~10s into the stream. snippet: 

```js
const stream = await client.funcall({
  name: 'parse_invoice',
  args: { text },
  stream: true,
});
for await (const chunk of stream) {
  // buffer and parse JSON chunks
}
// final: validate(result, InvoiceSchema) // throws
```

ken: two separate issues: 1) streaming framing + incremental JSON -> you need a tolerant parser that can handle partial chunks and trailing commas/newlines. 2) auth: if your access token expires mid-stream, the server returns 401 and the SDK's automatic retry may try to replay the request which is not safe for non-idempotent func-calls.

tariq: for (1) we used a small helper that accumulates `text` chunks and tries JSON.parse only when buffer looks complete. gist: try to parse on `

` boundary or look for balancing braces. also run zod on the *completed* object rather than on partial fragments. example: 

```js
function streamJsonAssembler(asyncIterable) {
  let buf = ''
  for await (const part of asyncIterable) {
    buf += part.text || part // depending on SDK shape
    // try parse when newline-separated or when braces match
    if (looksComplete(buf)) {
      const obj = JSON.parse(buf)
      yield obj
      buf = ''
    }
  }
  if (buf.trim()) { try { yield JSON.parse(buf) } catch(e) { /* last-chunk parse error */ } }
}
```

sam: FWIW we added a small SDK helper `funcSchemaShim` (internal) that wraps streams into a validated async iterator: it buffers until parseable, runs schema.validate, and attaches diagnostics for partial failures so callers can decide soft-fail vs hard-fail. Could do a PR if you want.

maria: that would help — currently we just call JSON.parse on each `data` chunk which is brittle.

yuki: for the auth bit: the streaming connection is long-lived. If your auth provider rotates tokens, you need either: pre-refresh before stream start (if you know expiry), or server-side support for a refresh token handshake mid-stream (rare). Our pattern: attach an `x-rdwd-retry-key` idempotency header and mark func-calls as non-retriable by default, then implement client-side reconnect with resume tokens only when the function is safe to replay.

ken: +1. also ensure the SDK's auth callback is invoked per-request (some configs cache token at client init). provide an `authProvider: async () => fetchFreshToken()` so each new connection gets a fresh token.

maria: how do we avoid automatic retry on 401 for streams? current SDK config has global `retries: 2`.

tariq: set `retries: 0` on the funcall route or mark route as `noReplay: true` in request meta. Example: 

```js
await client.funcall({ name: 'parse_invoice', args, stream: true, meta: { noReplay: true }, retries: 0 })
```

sam: another small tip — annotate every func-call invocation with `requestId` so if you *do* need to replay you can dedupe server-side. header: `x-rdwd-request-id`. serverside checks last-successful-step and either continue or reject.

maria: thanks — do folks want a short RFC on safer streaming semantics (buffered validation, auth refresh patterns, no-replay defaults for func-calls)? I can draft.

yuki: please do, include examples for JS + Python SDKs and a recommended `noReplay` default for any call that can mutate state.

ci-bot: CI: check-run queued for branch `maria/funcall-stream-fixes` — tests: unit streaming parser, auth-provider, no-replay flag.

maria: will open PR with helper + tests. i'll try `authProvider` + `funcSchemaShim` locally and report back. thanks all :thumbsup:
